## Description

[NestJS Framework](https://github.com/nestjs/nest) server project generated from official [JHipster NodeJS blueprint](https://github.com/jhipster/generator-jhipster-nodejs).

## Installation

```bash
$ npm install
```

## TypeORM configuration

### The database of each profile

The database of each profile is the `database` section of its configuration file, read by
[src/orm.config.ts](src/orm.config.ts) (the TypeORM options):

- [src/config/application-dev.yml](src/config/application-dev.yml): the database of development, the one of the
  `docker` folder<% if (databaseTypeSql) { %> (or a SQLite file)<% } %>;
- [src/config/application-test.yml](src/config/application-test.yml): <%= databaseTypeSql ? 'an in-memory SQLite database' : 'an in-memory MongoDB server' %> for the tests;
- [src/config/application-prod.yml](src/config/application-prod.yml): the production database, the service of the
  docker compose files by default.

```yaml
database:
  type: <%= nodeProdDatabaseType %>
  host: localhost
  port: 5432
  username: YOUR_USER
  password: 'YOUR_PASSWORD'
  name: YOUR_DATABASE_NAME
  logging: false
```

Like every property, an environment variable overrides them: `DATABASE_HOST`, `DATABASE_PORT`, `DATABASE_USERNAME`,
`DATABASE_PASSWORD`, `DATABASE_NAME`. Keep the production password out of the file this way.

### Configuration from environment variables

Like in a Spring Boot application, an environment variable overrides a property of the `src/config/application*.yml`
files: its name is the property in upper case, with the dots as underscores and the dashes removed (or as
underscores). For example, `JHIPSTER_MAIL_BASEURL` sets `jhipster.mail.base-url`. Secrets, like the JWT secret, can
be kept out of the configuration files this way in production.

### Migration data and schema

According [typeORM migration guide](https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/docs/migrations.md),
there are under [src/migrations/](src/migrations/) the scripts to create the database schema and after to insert data seed.
The scripts are automatically run in the first start up, and after anymore.

The `typeorm` npm scripts build the server and run the typeorm CLI on the database `BACKEND_ENV` selects (see
[ormconfig.ts](ormconfig.ts)):

```bash
<%_ if (databaseTypeSql) { _%>
# generate a migration from the changes of the entities
$ npm run typeorm:migration:generate
<%_ } _%>
# create an empty migration
$ npm run typeorm:migration:create
# run, revert or list the migrations
$ npm run typeorm:migration:run
$ npm run typeorm:migration:revert
$ npm run typeorm:migration:show
<%_ if (databaseTypeSql) { _%>
# show the SQL that would synchronize the schema with the entities
$ npm run typeorm:schema:log
<%_ } _%>
```
<%_ if (databaseTypeMongodb) { _%>

With MongoDB, run them with `BACKEND_ENV=prod` (the dev and test profiles use an in-memory server). MongoDB has no
schema to compare, so migrations are written by hand with the MongoDB API (`queryRunner.query()` does not support
MongoDB): use `queryRunner.dataSource.mongoManager`.
<%_ } _%>

## Running the app

```bash
# development
$ npm run start

# watch mode
$ npm run start:dev

# build transpiling files in javascript
$ npm run build

# run javascript build from the source project
$ npm run start:prod

# run javascript build with node
$ node dist/main.js
```

### Development profile and .env file

The development scripts (`npm start`, `npm run start:dev`, `npm run start:debug`) run with the **BACKEND_ENV** of the
[.env.dev](.env.dev) file, **dev**: the dev database and settings, the sample secrets and the API documentation. The
built application (`npm run start:prod`, `node dist/main.js`, the Docker image) does not read it, like a Spring Boot jar
built with the prod profile: set **BACKEND_ENV** where it runs, like `prod`.

An optional `.env` file, ignored by git, holds local settings, like secrets: the application loads it with
[dotenv](https://www.npmjs.com/package/dotenv) when it exists. A variable set in the environment wins over both files.

### Profiles

Like `spring.profiles.active` in a Spring Boot application, **BACKEND_ENV** is a comma separated list of profiles, for
example `prod,api-docs`. Each profile loads its `application-{profile}.yml` file of the [config folder](src/config), when
there is one, in the order of the list (the last one wins), and the profiles of its group (`profiles.group` of
[application.yml](src/config/application.yml)): `dev` activates `secret-samples` and `api-docs`, `test` activates
`secret-samples`. The standard profiles are:

- `dev`, `prod` and `test`: the database and the settings of each environment;
- `api-docs`: the OpenAPI document of the API at `/v3/api-docs`, for the administrators like JHipster Spring Boot<%= skipClient ? '' : ', read by the API page of the client' %>;
- `secret-samples`: samples of the secrets (<%= authenticationType === 'oauth2' ? 'the session secret and the OAuth2 client' : 'the JWT secret' %><%= databaseTypeSql ? ', the username and the password of the database' : '' %>) for development and CI, in
  [application-secret-samples.yml](src/config/application-secret-samples.yml). In production, set them with environment
  variables instead, like `<%= authenticationType === 'oauth2' ? 'JHIPSTER_SECURITY_OAUTH2_CLIENT_REGISTRATION_OIDC_CLIENTSECRET' : 'JHIPSTER_SECURITY_AUTHENTICATION_JWT_BASE64SECRET' %>`;
- `tls`: HTTPS, with your PKCS#12 keystore (`SERVER_SSL_KEY_STORE` and `SERVER_SSL_KEY_STORE_PASSWORD`);
- `tls-samples`: with `tls`, the self-signed sample keystore of [src/config/tls](src/config/tls), for development only.

> You can define your custom profiles, with an application-{profile}.yml file in your [config folder](src/config); an
> environment of its own needs the `database` section of that environment.

The `/management/info` endpoint gives the active profiles to the client, which shows its ribbon on the
`info.display-ribbon-on-profiles` ones.

**BACKEND_ENV** can also be set in the environment of the process.
For example:

```bash

# development in prod environment, with the API documentation and the sample secrets
# in linux
$ BACKEND_ENV=prod,api-docs,secret-samples npm run start
# in windows
$ set BACKEND_ENV=prod,api-docs,secret-samples&& npm run start

# run javascript build with node in prod environment
# in linux
$ BACKEND_ENV=prod node dist/main.js
# in windows
$ set BACKEND_ENV=prod&& node dist/main.js
```

## Lint

```bash
# run lint
$ npm run lint

# fix lint issues
$ npm run lint:fix

```

## Debug

```bash
# run this and after you can execute debug task in VSCode
$ npm run start:debug

```

## Test

```bash
# unit tests
$ npm run test

# lint
$ npm run lint

# fix lint issues
$ npm run lint:fix

# test coverage of unit tests
$ npm run test:cov

# e2e tests with full app coverage report
$ npm run test:e2e

```

